      "question": "<strong>Case Study:</strong> A manufacturer has 600 litres of a 12% solution of acid. How many litres of a 30% acid solution must be added to it so that acid content in the resulting mixture will be more than 15% but less than 18%?",
      "finalAnswer": "Between 120 and 300 litres",
      "solutionSteps": [
        "Let $x$ litres be added. Total mixture = $600+x$.",
        "Total acid = $0.12(600) + 0.30x = 72 + 0.3x$.",
        "Condition 1: $\\frac{72+0.3x}{600+x} > 0.15 \\implies 72+0.3x > 90+0.15x \\implies 0.15x > 18 \\implies x > 120$.",
        "Condition 2: $\\frac{72+0.3x}{600+x} < 0.18 \\implies 72+0.3x < 108+0.18x \\implies 0.12x < 36 \\implies x < 300$.",
        "Range: $120 < x < 300$."
